  "caveats": [
   "One sequence. It is the test sequence with the largest change between its first and last map, so it is the least typical one in that respect (its difference is 2.0 times the test median).",
   "The top-row frame (63) is also the last frame of the bottom row, so the bottom row's last panel repeats it with the hand in place.",
   "In frames 0, 8 and 16 the hand is beside the handle and not closed on it (nearest hand vertex 6.4 mm from the knife at frame 0); the faint colour there is proximity, not touch. The study starts a sequence at the first frame of 'firm contact', which it defines as 'n_hard>0 and mass>=zero_mass' (a vertex within 1 cm of the hand, amount at least 1.5) for 3 frames in a row; frame 0 meets it without a grasp.",
   "The picture is coarse twice over: the value decays over 2 cm, and the 512-value map is spread back onto the mesh by the study's own operator, which smooths. It shows that the whole handle is in contact, not which finger touches where, and the colour reaches a little onto the blade next to the handle.",
   "The 512 points come from the category's template knife (mesh 122, blade 0.068 m high against 0.093 m here) scaled to this knife, not from this knife: their outline is another knife's, and they lie up to 14.3 mm from this knife's nearest mesh vertex (median 2.9 mm).",
   "Frames 0, 8 and 16 look almost alike: the hand hardly moves before it closes on the handle.",
   "The opaque hand hides the part of the handle under the fingers in the bottom row; the top-middle panel shows that frame's map without the hand.",
   "No model output is shown; TACO only."
  ],
  "suggested_caption": "One recorded TACO test sequence, a right hand taking hold of a knife, selected as the test sequence whose contact map differs most between its first and its last frame (rank 1 of 364), so it is not a typical one. Top row, frame 63 (the frame of this sequence with the largest contact amount): the recorded hand on the knife; the knife alone with that frame's contact map drawn on its surface; and the same map as it is stored, one value on each of 512 points. The points come from a template knife of the category, scaled to this knife, so their outline differs from it; the pale shape behind them is this knife. Bottom row: frames 0, 8, 16, 32, 48 and 63 of the 64, with the hand. The value falls off with the distance to the hand (scale 2 cm) instead of switching on at touch, so at frame 0, when the hand is still beside the handle (6.4 mm from the knife at its nearest point), the handle is already faintly coloured, and the colour deepens as the hand closes on it. All panels share one colour scale, 0 to 1.",
  "suggested_alt": "Renders of one recorded sequence of a right hand grasping a knife: the hand on the knife, the knife with the contact coloured on its handle, the same contact as coloured dots, and six frames over time in which the colour on the handle deepens as the hand closes."
 }
